33ac197c12ad"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "A vertex identifier denotes one vertex, so it can carry only one label. The \
explicit vertex list wins if the two disagree, and the disagreement is \
reported rather than resolved silently. This keeps the invariant that the \
vertex set is the single source of truth about labels, which is what makes \
labelled isomorphism well defined."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"85c50da9-646d-d844-ae7c-2a1c88359b9e"],

Cell["A hypergraph is a multiset of edges", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"d1af2ecc-8c5d-8044-a846-cde6fdc2c40a"],

Cell["\<\
Edge order carries no information, so the two spellings below are the same \
object:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7f462650-a7af-e745-a21d-9bdc3ecce5cd"],

Cell["\<\
Hypergraph[{Edge[{1, 2}], Edge[{2, 3}]}] === Hypergraph[{Edge[{2, 3}], \
Edge[{1, 2}]}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"1c6901a8-51ac-644b-b515-dfb91c365434"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"839d0b98-6283-df45-9f0b-431b737fd498"],

Cell["\<\
Multiplicity, on the other hand, does carry information. A repeated edge is \
kept, is drawn as a second arc, and a hypergraph with a doubled edge is not \
the same as one without:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"53a7cc07-2ef2-e249-8c7c-0c60867928fd"],

Cell["Hypergraph[{Edge[{1, 2}], Edge[{1, 2}], Edge[{2, 3}]}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"9c8fba4e-70e2-ee40-a050-abe5ec848b5b"],

